   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to the reportable\ntick-borne disease of Alpha-gal syndrome",
    "basePrintNo": "S10340",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10340-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §206-c, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10340\n\nSPONSOR: MAY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to the reportable\ntick-borne disease of Alpha-gal syndrome\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require the reporting of Alpha-gal syndrome cases to better track its\nspread and coordinate a public health response in New York.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill amends public health law to require the Commissioner of Health\nto promulgate rules and regulations establishing Alpha-gal syndrome as a\nreportable disease. The bill also requires the Commissioner to expand\nthe list of tick-borne diseases included within the tick-borne disease\nsurveillance report to include Alpha-gal syndrome.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAlpha-gal syndrome has emerged as a life changing condition caused by\nthe bite from a Lone Star tick which is prevalent across New York State.\nThe syndrome can cause individuals to develop new allergic reactions to\nred meat and other mammal products. Some people have died because of\nallergic reactions caused by this syndrome. Diagnosis is difficult as\nthere is no single definitive test. Currently, a blood test for specific\nantibodies to Alpha-gal is the primary means of confirmation.\n\nNew York, specifically Long Island, is a hot spot for Alpha-gal\nsyndrome. According to the CDC, Suffolk County appears to have more\ncases of Alpha-gal than any other county in the United States. The CDC\nstudy also suggests that between 3,800 and 18,000 people in Suffolk\nCounty have the syndrome. Despite this New York does not currently\nrequire the reporting of cases and therefore lacks necessary data to\nproperly respond to the spread of this syndrome across the state.\n\nOther hot spots include Arkansas, Kentucky, and Virginia; all three of\nthose states now require the reporting of any positive Alpha-gal\nsyndrome test. In addition, New York City tracks cases of the syndrome\ndespite it not being prevalent there.\n\nMaking Alpha-gal syndrome a reportable condition will allow the Depart-\nment of Health to collect data on the syndrome including where hotspots\nare and to use that data to better respond to its spread.  Reporting\nwill also support healthcare providers in diagnosing the condition more\neffectively.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n180 days after enactment\n",
